Description: antinomie: debug engine reads a thread's registers only while its stack is held
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Origin: project
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-22

A thread's user registers are on its kernel stack. The engine holds a reference on each thread's task_struct but not on its stack, which an exiting thread frees at its last switch (VMAP_STACK), leaving task->stack NULL. A stopped group that was killed (a timer_create() SIGKILL while the debugger looked at a breakpoint stop) left its threads' records running and the session STOPPED; Continue then read the reporting thread's instruction pointer through task_pt_regs() and oopsed the debug worker, which ended every debug session until reboot. A stepped thread that exited in the step (a syscall instruction with rax = exit) had its trap flag cleared the same way.  Every access to another thread's registers (an event's pc, the thread list, REGS_READ and REGS_WRITE, the resume past a hardware breakpoint, the step's arm and its undo) now holds the stack with try_get_task_stack() and finds nothing (-ESRCH, pc 0) once it is gone; a step of a thread that left skips the trap-flag undo. Continue first asks whether the group is over, and STATUS does for a stopped session too, so a killed group reads as exited. Continue does not touch a thread that left. The contract revision stays 17.

